cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  timetransfer: clock offset and skew across a link, with the uncertainty
  measured rather than quoted
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-24
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Two-way time transfer that estimates clock offset, skew and drift from
  four-timestamp exchanges under asymmetric and variable delay, reports
  stability as an overlapping Allan deviation over tau with a confidence
  interval at each tau, and attaches a measured uncertainty to the offset
  estimate itself rather than a nominal figure from a datasheet. The analytic
  baseline is the symmetric-delay estimator, whose asymmetry-induced bias is
  derived in closed form as half the one-way delay difference and verified to a
  worst departure of 1.084e-19 seconds across a declared asymmetry sweep with a
  fitted slope of 0.500000000000; the bias is shown to be independent of the
  one-way delay, the turnaround and the true offset, and to survive averaging,
  which is the identifiability result made numerical. Least-squares and
  two-state Kalman estimation of offset and skew follow, with the skew error
  converging at a fitted exponent of -1.5192 plus or minus 0.0218 against the
  theoretical minus three halves. The Allan deviation is implemented in the
  package rather than taken from allantools, which exists on the index at
  version 2024.6 but is not installed in the environment this product was
  measured in, and is verified against three closed-form noise processes with
  fitted slopes of -0.9998, -0.4834 and +0.5194 against theory of -1, -1/2 and
  +1/2; the measured coverage of its confidence interval ranges from 0.9233 to
  0.9767 across twenty-seven averaging-time cells against a nominal 0.95, and
  that shortfall is published. The uncertainty budget combines a statistical
  term inflated for serial correlation measured from the fit residuals, a
  quantisation term equal to the timestamp resolution over the square root of
  twelve, and an asymmetry term that is either omitted, bounded from external
  knowledge, or taken from a corrector's residual spread measured on a
  calibration split. Its coverage at a coverage factor of 1.96 against a
  nominal 0.9500 is 0.1200 with no asymmetry term, 0.9900 with a declared
  bound, 0.9450 with a median-filtered corrector and 0.9525 with a learned one,
  over four hundred held-out scenarios. The learned delay-asymmetry corrector
  is benchmarked against the symmetric assumption, a median-filtered estimator,
  a mean-difference estimator and a linear ridge on the same sixteen window
  features: the gradient-boosted model loses to the ridge at every generator
  coupling measured, by root-mean-square-error ratios of 1.0273, 1.0099 and
  1.0088, and its own quantile-pair uncertainty band under-covers at 0.8917
  against 0.9500, so the model card recommends against it on its own
  measurements. The median filter matches the learned model to within four per
  cent where the generator makes the static asymmetry unlearnable and loses by
  forty-four and eighty-nine per cent where it does not. The product carries
  the full Level 4 scaffolding: a hardware abstraction layer whose simulated
  and device backends produce byte-identical timestamps and an offset maximum
  difference of zero across four cases with a falsifying control that differs;
  a seeded simulation mode reproducible at seven separate levels; a dry-run
  mode replaying a committed recording with zero hardware operations and a
  negative control firing two hundred and forty-one refusals; a documented
  Jetson Orin Nano deployment and recovery procedure whose ten pre-flight
  checks pass and whose five injected aborts all restore cleanly; and a
  benchmark harness whose five Level 4 hardware rows are empty and marked
  unmeasured, protected by three guards that raise rather than permit a row to
  be filled from simulation. Nothing in the repository has run on a board,
  every backend reports is_hardware false, and the validation label is Level 3,
  hardware-pending and is never Level 4 on simulated numbers. This is a
  measurement and characterisation tool, not a time-synchronisation daemon;
  linuxptp and chrony are named as the mature answer for actually
  synchronising clocks. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified,
  not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - time-transfer
  - clock-offset
  - clock-skew
  - allan-deviation
  - delay-asymmetry
  - measurement-uncertainty
  - hardware-in-the-loop
  - two-way-time-transfer
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Mills
        given-names: "D. L."
    title: "Internet time synchronization: the Network Time Protocol"
    journal: "IEEE Transactions on Communications"
    volume: 39
    issue: 10
    year: 1991
    notes: >-
      Section III gives the offset and delay formulas implemented in
      timetransfer.twoway as equations (3) and (4), and the symmetric-delay
      assumption whose cost this package quantifies.
  - type: standard
    title: >-
      IEEE Standard for a Precision Clock Synchronization Protocol for
      Networked Measurement and Control Systems
    institution:
      name: "IEEE"
    number: "IEEE Std 1588-2019"
    year: 2019
    notes: >-
      Section 7.4.2, mean path delay. The same symmetric assumption, and the
      reason linuxptp is named in the README as the mature answer for
      synchronisation rather than characterisation.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Allan
        given-names: "D. W."
    title: "Statistics of atomic frequency standards"
    journal: "Proceedings of the IEEE"
    volume: 54
    issue: 2
    year: 1966
    notes: The two-sample variance implemented in timetransfer.allan.
  - type: report
    authors:
      - family-names: Riley
        given-names: "W. J."
    title: "Handbook of Frequency Stability Analysis"
    institution:
      name: "National Institute of Standards and Technology"
    number: "NIST Special Publication 1065"
    year: 2008
    notes: >-
      Section 5.2.4 for the overlapping Allan variance, Table 3 for the
      simplified equivalent-degrees-of-freedom formulas after Howe, Allan and
      Barnes 1981, and section 5.5.3 for the lag-1 autocorrelation noise
      identification method. The measured coverage of the resulting intervals,
      0.9233 to 0.9767 against a nominal 0.95, is published in
      validation/VALIDATION.md.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Zucca
        given-names: "C."
      - family-names: Tavella
        given-names: "P."
    title: >-
      The clock model and its relationship with the Allan and related variances
    journal: >-
      IEEE Transactions on Ultrasonics, Ferroelectrics and Frequency Control
    volume: 52
    issue: 2
    year: 2005
    notes: >-
      The two-state clock model, its discrete-time process covariance used by
      timetransfer.skew, and the identity sigma_y^2(tau) = q1/tau + q2 tau/3
      verified numerically in validation/validate_allan.py.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Bennett
        given-names: "W. R."
    title: "Spectra of quantized signals"
    journal: "Bell System Technical Journal"
    volume: 27
    issue: 3
    year: 1948
    notes: >-
      The uniform quantisation variance delta^2/12 behind the quantisation term
      of the offset uncertainty budget.
  - type: standard
    title: >-
      Evaluation of measurement data: Guide to the expression of uncertainty in
      measurement
    institution:
      name: "Joint Committee for Guides in Metrology"
    number: "JCGM 100:2008"
    year: 2008
    notes: >-
      Section 4.3.7 for the type B evaluation from a bound, giving
      (B/2)/sqrt(3), and section 5.1.2 for combination in quadrature.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Bayley
        given-names: "G. V."
      - family-names: Hammersley
        given-names: "J. M."
    title: >-
      The effective number of independent observations in an autocorrelated
      time series
    journal: "Journal of the Royal Statistical Society, Supplement"
    volume: 8
    issue: 2
    year: 1946
    notes: >-
      The AR(1) effective sample size n(1-r1)/(1+r1) used to inflate the
      statistical term of the offset uncertainty.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Clopper
        given-names: "C. J."
      - family-names: Pearson
        given-names: "E. S."
    title: >-
      The use of confidence or fiducial limits illustrated in the case of the
      binomial
    journal: "Biometrika"
    volume: 26
    issue: 4
    year: 1934
    notes: The exact interval placed on every measured coverage fraction.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Rousseeuw
        given-names: "P. J."
      - family-names: Croux
        given-names: "C."
    title: "Alternatives to the median absolute deviation"
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 88
    issue: 424
    year: 1993
    notes: >-
      The Gaussian consistency factor applied to the median absolute deviation
      in measurement_variance_from_data. Because the queueing delay is a
      right-skewed gamma rather than a Gaussian, the resulting Kalman filter is
      measured over-confident with a mean normalised innovation of 2.53 to
      2.65, and that is reported rather than tuned away.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Friedman
        given-names: "J. H."
    title: "Greedy function approximation: a gradient boosting machine"
    journal: "Annals of Statistics"
    volume: 29
    issue: 5
    year: 2001
    notes: >-
      The gradient-boosted asymmetry corrector and its quantile variant. It
      loses to a standardised linear ridge on the same features at every
      generator coupling measured.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Draper
        given-names: "N. R."
      - family-names: Smith
        given-names: "H."
    title: "Applied Regression Analysis"
    edition: "3rd"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 1998
    notes: >-
      Chapter 5 for the least-squares parameter covariance sigma^2 (X'X)^-1 and
      for the slope variance that gives the n^-3/2 convergence rate checked in
      validation/validate_skew_estimators.py.
